121
00:11:39,188 --> 00:11:44,411
[SPEAKER_08]: I think it's important to talk a little bit about a lot of the changes that happened for the Metro Police Department in the year 2021.

122
00:11:45,772 --> 00:11:49,734
[SPEAKER_08]: We've serviced over 36,000 calls for service.

123
00:11:49,754 --> 00:11:51,915
[SPEAKER_08]: That's the highest in about five years.

124
00:11:53,136 --> 00:11:56,178
[SPEAKER_08]: We continue to get calls and increase calls and continue to respond.

125
00:11:57,276 --> 00:12:04,522
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm fond of saying we do more with less, and I think that's everybody in this country these days, but it continues, and I'm pleased to comment.

126
00:12:05,343 --> 00:12:08,405
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm proud to say that in the past, we've had a Health and Human Services.

127
00:12:08,445 --> 00:12:08,906
[SPEAKER_08]: You've heard us.

128
00:12:09,186 --> 00:12:16,332
[SPEAKER_08]: We've partnered with the Office of Prevention and Outreach, but we were able to create a new behavioral support unit.

129
00:12:17,272 --> 00:12:20,595
[SPEAKER_08]: So originally, our Health and Human Services was a police officer trained in

130
00:12:21,996 --> 00:12:26,842
[SPEAKER_08]: not just crisis intervention training, but a lot of mental health services, but we had a full time clinician on board.

131
00:12:27,823 --> 00:12:29,565
[SPEAKER_08]: We were seeing a lot of success with that.

132
00:12:30,606 --> 00:12:35,232
[SPEAKER_08]: And I petitioned the Department of Mental Health who have a large jail diversion grant that they

133
00:12:35,643 --> 00:12:38,704
[SPEAKER_08]: They have a strong support for what they call co-response program.

134
00:12:39,144 --> 00:12:58,392
[SPEAKER_08]: And after numerous conversations with the Department of Mental Health and based on the state funding, we in the city were able to obtain 291,000 annual grant to continue that program, but to increase it to two co-response clinicians and one full-time youth and family clinician.

135
00:12:59,652 --> 00:13:01,773
[SPEAKER_08]: I can't tell you how happy we are.

136
00:13:02,673 --> 00:13:07,737
[SPEAKER_08]: with this program and we expect it to grow even more as the years go forward.

137
00:13:08,097 --> 00:13:10,218
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a multi-year grant at that dollar value.

138
00:13:11,599 --> 00:13:14,241
[SPEAKER_08]: The two new clinicians will be starting next week.

139
00:13:14,902 --> 00:13:19,545
[SPEAKER_08]: And so this next fiscal year will show a big change and a big impact for this.

140
00:13:20,045 --> 00:13:25,549
[SPEAKER_08]: And essentially this behavioral support unit deals with mental health and substance use disorders.

141
00:13:25,709 --> 00:13:29,632
[SPEAKER_08]: A large percentage of our calls revolve around that.

142
00:13:30,591 --> 00:13:48,673
[SPEAKER_08]: The difference right now is that they can a clinician can either respond with a police officer, or we can respond, we can see that this is not necessarily a police matter it's a mental health matter, or a other sort of crisis, and we can have a clinician dispatch to the scene and they can deal with the situation.

143
00:13:49,073 --> 00:13:55,277
[SPEAKER_08]: And in some instances, in many instances, I could say that we would just leave, we just walk away and the clinicians do all the work they do all the follow up.

144
00:13:56,077 --> 00:14:01,040
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a direct access to clinical support through Elliot human services.

145
00:14:02,061 --> 00:14:08,724
[SPEAKER_08]: And it's hard these days to find clinical support for different things.

146
00:14:08,825 --> 00:14:14,848
[SPEAKER_08]: And when we show up at your house, and we're able to give you a clinician to speak to you right away, and they can reach out to Elliot services and create

147
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:22,233
[SPEAKER_08]: that direct contract, we see a significant improvement in what we're trying to do and reach out.

148
00:14:23,794 --> 00:14:28,115
[SPEAKER_08]: Earlier today, I talked about the advances in leadership in the police department.

149
00:14:28,576 --> 00:14:30,857
[SPEAKER_08]: We continue to provide extensive training.

150
00:14:32,137 --> 00:14:37,079
[SPEAKER_08]: Good leadership is good policing and provides good support for the police officers.

151
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:40,581
[SPEAKER_08]: So we have pushed for this, but

152
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:45,781
[SPEAKER_08]: One of the interesting things that we've changed in this past year is we've created assessment centers.

153
00:14:45,841 --> 00:14:47,161
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a civil service process.

154
00:14:47,662 --> 00:15:00,365
[SPEAKER_08]: In the past, police leaders were promoted or defined simply by, you would go to a statewide examination center, you'd take a test and you'd get ranked on the test, and we would pick you based on how you ranked on that test.

155
00:15:01,245 --> 00:15:04,346
[SPEAKER_08]: The department itself had no real input on those tests.

156
00:15:05,146 --> 00:15:06,786
[SPEAKER_08]: And again, there was standard statewide.

157
00:15:07,246 --> 00:15:08,947
[SPEAKER_08]: The assessment center now, it's,

158
00:15:10,865 --> 00:15:16,627
[SPEAKER_08]: We're able to put forth candidates who to undergo real world applicable scenarios.

159
00:15:17,467 --> 00:15:26,631
[SPEAKER_08]: And we're able to test the decision making abilities were able to test the quality of the decisions and their ability essentially to lead during crisis and other times and how they handle.

160
00:15:27,131 --> 00:15:29,732
[SPEAKER_08]: And it is, it's, you know, we expect to see.

161
00:15:31,095 --> 00:15:32,736
[SPEAKER_08]: the future of the police department.

162
00:15:32,816 --> 00:15:37,480
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, the idea is if any leader sits in one place, you should be reinforcing the place.

163
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,983
[SPEAKER_08]: So when it's my time to go, somebody else can step up and lead.

164
00:15:40,303 --> 00:15:43,145
[SPEAKER_08]: And so when lieutenants have time, a new lieutenant steps in the role.

165
00:15:43,726 --> 00:15:48,510
[SPEAKER_08]: And so investing in leadership is a priority of mine, and we continue to do it.

166
00:15:49,390 --> 00:15:53,734
[SPEAKER_08]: I mentioned to you that 2020 was a real difficult year for hiring police officers.

167
00:15:53,774 --> 00:15:55,155
[SPEAKER_08]: Essentially, there were no academies, right?

168
00:15:55,175 --> 00:15:57,918
[SPEAKER_08]: So you couldn't, even if you wanted to hire them, and COVID was a strike.

169
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:00,100
[SPEAKER_08]: We hire 20 police officers.

170
00:16:01,161 --> 00:16:06,567
[SPEAKER_08]: They're an incredible group of officers, this Friday and Saturday we graduate two more classes.

171
00:16:08,228 --> 00:16:15,596
[SPEAKER_08]: We have a young department and they're full of energy, and it's a, you know, these are just general nice men and women who

172
00:16:16,477 --> 00:16:18,737
[SPEAKER_08]: who just cannot wait to serve their communities.

173
00:16:18,937 --> 00:16:21,118
[SPEAKER_08]: And I see this as a clear change, right?

174
00:16:21,138 --> 00:16:23,398
[SPEAKER_08]: They're coming in with different attitudes, the academies are changing.

175
00:16:23,878 --> 00:16:40,361
[SPEAKER_08]: And I say to you that the investment in new offices and these younger generation offices, it's just an investment in the future of this community too, because they look at things like, I'm not that old of a person, but age changes things and they move forward.

176
00:16:40,381 --> 00:16:44,682
[SPEAKER_08]: So I'm very, very happy at being able to continue to support that.

177
00:16:45,669 --> 00:16:49,672
[SPEAKER_08]: There's three systems we introduced into the police department to change our management of the police department.

178
00:16:49,692 --> 00:16:51,093
[SPEAKER_08]: The first one is called PMAM.

179
00:16:52,755 --> 00:16:56,137
[SPEAKER_08]: It's essentially a professional management system for our policy and documentation.

180
00:16:56,498 --> 00:17:02,883
[SPEAKER_08]: Anyone who's listened to me publicly speak about the police department told me we're trying to, I've heard you say, I'm trying to redo all our policies and procedures.

181
00:17:03,904 --> 00:17:09,988
[SPEAKER_08]: In theory, there should be about 250, 260 policies and procedures for an accredited police department.

182
00:17:10,268 --> 00:17:11,169
[SPEAKER_08]: We're not there yet.

183
00:17:11,249 --> 00:17:19,936
[SPEAKER_08]: It takes a lot of time and effort to do it, but the PMAM system allows us to sort of put them in a library, document them, send them out during different shifts for training.

184
00:17:20,176 --> 00:17:27,962
[SPEAKER_08]: We can monitor the police officers as they're following the policies, make sure they understand and they can respond back to us with questions and policies and just a clear cut.

185
00:17:29,003 --> 00:17:40,049
[SPEAKER_08]: We've also introduced another new system software called I a pro I pro is, you know, is associated exactly what it sounds like it's documents records and reviews all of our internal incidents and our internal affairs systems.

186
00:17:41,210 --> 00:17:47,994
[SPEAKER_08]: But it provides something that is new and software we call it early intervention right so it can start the flag if you see certain offices who

187
00:17:48,478 --> 00:18:09,628
[SPEAKER_08]: may come up, you know, like this issue this issue and you say well that's an anomaly and it sends an email to somebody to check and make sure that, you know, a that that office is okay but that somebody supervisory level should be putting eyes on what's going on with those police officers to prevent anything in the future from going bad, many police departments across the country have had success with that program.

188
00:18:10,537 --> 00:18:12,558
[SPEAKER_08]: Coupled with that we have something called blue team.

189
00:18:13,218 --> 00:18:19,440
[SPEAKER_08]: And the best way to sort of describe blue team is it's an upgrade to a street level reporting systems.

190
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:21,321
[SPEAKER_08]: So for police officer goes out and use.

191
00:18:22,881 --> 00:18:27,103
[SPEAKER_08]: Let's say a use of force situation right they write a police report, but they also have to do a use of force for

192
00:18:27,550 --> 00:18:30,971
[SPEAKER_08]: That historically has been a piece of paper that gets stored in a folder somewhere.

193
00:18:31,351 --> 00:18:33,332
[SPEAKER_08]: Now we're electronically documenting it all.

194
00:18:33,432 --> 00:18:46,635
[SPEAKER_08]: And we'll be able to, through the software, just sort of have a better look and a better view of what we're doing when it comes to use of force, complaints, and other performance matters for police officers.

195
00:18:46,695 --> 00:18:48,736
[SPEAKER_08]: It sort of puts it all on a dashboard for us to see 2022.

196
00:18:49,836 --> 00:18:55,618
[SPEAKER_08]: And we're able to look at this differently than trying to pull pieces of paper out of folders and put it all together in comparison.

197
00:18:56,378 --> 00:18:57,879
[SPEAKER_08]: community engagement.

198
00:18:57,899 --> 00:19:15,071
[SPEAKER_08]: Um we've also since the summertime created um one of the things that I'm very proud of is uh community engagement unit and this is a group of officers who essentially volunteering to step up and engage themselves in our community through both social media and a number of community events.

