[SPEAKER_12]: Keep it on the vehicles for a second.

694
00:57:44,080 --> 00:57:47,782
[SPEAKER_12]: These may be coming from President Morrell, who isn't here tonight.

695
00:57:48,781 --> 00:57:53,123
[SPEAKER_08]: update looking at your phone's not giving it away.

696
00:57:53,303 --> 00:57:55,524
[SPEAKER_12]: Well, it was even even you've heard these before.

697
00:57:55,544 --> 00:57:59,126
[SPEAKER_12]: Update on potentially moving to electric vehicles.

698
00:58:00,147 --> 00:58:01,828
[SPEAKER_08]: So we continue to study this issue, right?

699
00:58:02,048 --> 00:58:04,189
[SPEAKER_08]: And I don't think anyone's sort of averse to this.

700
00:58:04,249 --> 00:58:09,852
[SPEAKER_08]: I think I think I'll tell you where my mind is right now that we very much move to hybrid vehicles.

701
00:58:10,852 --> 00:58:12,533
[SPEAKER_08]: Just think about some of the conversations we've had.

702
00:58:12,573 --> 00:58:13,914
[SPEAKER_08]: We are running at 24 hours a day.

703
00:58:14,094 --> 00:58:16,115
[SPEAKER_08]: I just asked the question, when do we charge?

704
00:58:17,035 --> 00:58:21,759
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah, take some time to charge these vehicles and we in an all electric vehicle.

705
00:58:22,660 --> 00:58:28,304
[SPEAKER_08]: I don't, I don't, I don't see the times that we could fit them in and charge them and we certainly don't have the charging stations in place.

706
00:58:28,625 --> 00:58:33,909
[SPEAKER_08]: I see Mike in the monitor want to say something, but hybrids are, you know, I know that

707
00:58:34,600 --> 00:58:39,081
[SPEAKER_08]: A lot of police departments, Boston State, other police departments around there, they're enjoying them.

708
00:58:39,161 --> 00:58:40,141
[SPEAKER_08]: There's not much of an issue.

709
00:58:40,241 --> 00:58:42,162
[SPEAKER_12]: And there's potential cost savings there as well.

710
00:58:42,322 --> 00:58:43,462
[SPEAKER_08]: Potential cost savings, yes.

711
00:58:43,682 --> 00:58:50,543
[SPEAKER_09]: So I believe Weston or Wayland, they got a Mustang Mach-E and they were flashing it all over Facebook.

712
00:58:51,043 --> 00:58:53,224
[SPEAKER_09]: Look at us, look at us.

713
00:58:54,324 --> 00:58:55,944
[SPEAKER_09]: I immediately received a phone call.

714
00:58:55,984 --> 00:58:57,585
[SPEAKER_09]: Look at this, look at this, look at this.

715
00:58:58,005 --> 00:59:03,586
[SPEAKER_09]: The problem with electric vehicles right now for our fleet, you will have to purchase three of them.

716
00:59:04,286 --> 00:59:08,548
[SPEAKER_09]: to replace one cruiser because they run 24 hours a day.

717
00:59:10,550 --> 00:59:12,331
[SPEAKER_09]: Financially, I don't think we can do that.

718
00:59:13,111 --> 00:59:29,440
[SPEAKER_09]: So me personally, and I had this conversation with the chief, undercover cars, like my cruiser, his cruiser, people that don't run them 24 hours, absolutely, we could go the electric vehicle route.

719
00:59:29,860 --> 00:59:32,842
[SPEAKER_09]: But for a black and white every day, I do not recommend it.

720
00:59:33,462 --> 00:59:34,863
[SPEAKER_09]: simply for the charging time.

721
00:59:35,263 --> 00:59:43,708
[SPEAKER_09]: They have come a long way with fast charges and all kinds of stuff to get them charged within an hour or so, or two hours.

722
00:59:44,149 --> 00:59:49,072
[SPEAKER_09]: But the lead time on that, you'd still have to replace one cruiser with two electric vehicles.

723
00:59:49,792 --> 00:59:55,235
[SPEAKER_09]: So I just, I don't think the technology's there yet to be able to put them in the black and white fleet.

724
00:59:57,306 --> 01:00:08,976
[SPEAKER_09]: The chief and I have discussed and I've priced out the Explorer hybrids extensively and I'm still talking to one of my vendors about purchasing those.

725
01:00:09,616 --> 01:00:14,460
[SPEAKER_09]: So we're strongly leading towards that route as to get into the electric game.

726
01:00:15,161 --> 01:00:28,445
[SPEAKER_09]: But for the most part, we also agreed that the fire chief, the police chief, things like that, the detectives, they would be the ones that we would start off with the electric vehicles because they don't want all the time.

727
01:00:29,326 --> 01:00:30,306
[SPEAKER_12]: OK, thank you.

728
01:00:30,346 --> 01:00:30,766
[SPEAKER_12]: That's helpful.

729
01:00:30,786 --> 01:00:33,507
[SPEAKER_12]: And I appreciate all the research and work that you put into looking into it.

730
01:00:34,258 --> 01:00:45,687
[SPEAKER_08]: And just a final point that if available and we weren't in dire straits and had to go to all gasoline, I had myself would buy hybrid vehicles right now.

731
01:00:45,767 --> 01:00:47,709
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, I just think that this will go.

732
01:00:48,129 --> 01:00:55,735
[SPEAKER_08]: As for Weston and Concord where I was out driving their electric vehicles, they have Mustangs and Teslas and they were donated by community members.

733
01:00:55,935 --> 01:00:57,256
[SPEAKER_08]: So it's just- Wow.

734
01:00:57,316 --> 01:01:00,879
[SPEAKER_08]: Wouldn't that be nice?

735
01:01:01,019 --> 01:01:01,219
[SPEAKER_08]: Yes.

736
01:01:03,989 --> 01:01:04,430
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you.

737
01:01:04,970 --> 01:01:13,916
[SPEAKER_12]: We've talked about this before I think I asked it last year regarding the shift from the sedan to the SUV and I understand that's basically the only thing that's being made at this point.

738
01:01:14,136 --> 01:01:14,376
[SPEAKER_08]: Correct.

739
01:01:14,817 --> 01:01:23,643
[SPEAKER_12]: But one other question is around around trucks, you know, I've all I've seen the, I think one that we have now is there more than one pickup truck that we're

740
01:01:24,103 --> 01:01:31,809
[SPEAKER_08]: We have one pickup truck and we've request requested through this casino mitigation fund grant for another pickup truck.

741
01:01:32,089 --> 01:01:40,536
[SPEAKER_08]: I'll be very clear that that we finding we're finding right now that in advancing our traffic sort of enforcement to be able to put signs and speed trails.

742
01:01:40,756 --> 01:01:42,798
[SPEAKER_08]: We have four speed trails now and actually the

743
01:01:43,320 --> 01:01:46,803
[SPEAKER_08]: pickup truck we're asking for the casino and the mitigation fund would come with another speed trail.

744
01:01:47,183 --> 01:01:53,489
[SPEAKER_08]: These sign boards and the speed trails have a vast, vast impact on traffic in our community of a lot of value.

745
01:01:54,230 --> 01:02:01,416
[SPEAKER_08]: And it's hard to take line cruises a transporter number one, I don't think it does a value to the crew, I think it's killing the life of those of these

746
01:02:02,477 --> 01:02:03,938
[SPEAKER_08]: explore cruises.

747
01:02:03,978 --> 01:02:05,259
[SPEAKER_08]: And so you need a little bit more.

748
01:02:05,399 --> 01:02:11,802
[SPEAKER_08]: And that's really where the design our current pickup truck is assigned to our traffic unit for the express purpose.

749
01:02:12,443 --> 01:02:13,043
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a cruiser.

750
01:02:13,063 --> 01:02:17,806
[SPEAKER_08]: So that helps, but it's for the express purpose of transporting our traffic tools around.

751
01:02:18,726 --> 01:02:19,847
[SPEAKER_08]: We need a delivery vehicle.

752
01:02:20,127 --> 01:02:21,148
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, essentially what it's for.

753
01:02:21,388 --> 01:02:25,170
[SPEAKER_08]: We do not intend to mine pickup trucks for every one of our cruises.

754
01:02:25,510 --> 01:02:28,912
[SPEAKER_08]: The casino mitigation will be strictly if we granted that,

755
01:02:29,272 --> 01:02:45,229
[SPEAKER_08]: it will be strictly for the same purposes for traffic assignments for putting signboards and moving I mean cones and barriers and other things and you know we hope to increase through some other grant sources more visible signs and to enhance traffic safety.

756
01:02:45,270 --> 01:02:50,896
[SPEAKER_08]: So that's the purpose of the pickup trucks not to change our whole fleet to a larger pick up a fleet of trucks and vehicles.

757
01:02:52,035 --> 01:02:52,515
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Chief.

758
01:02:52,795 --> 01:02:54,777
[SPEAKER_12]: And I do apologize for the temperature in the room.

759
01:02:54,797 --> 01:02:56,037
[SPEAKER_12]: I can see people are a little cold.

760
01:02:57,318 --> 01:03:00,960
[SPEAKER_12]: The air conditioning system, I mean, God knows who, how old it is, but it blows that way.

761
01:03:01,461 --> 01:03:02,842
[SPEAKER_12]: So we don't even get it.

762
01:03:02,902 --> 01:03:05,203
[SPEAKER_12]: We're sitting here in like five degrees warmer than everyone behind us.

763
01:03:05,223 --> 01:03:10,286
[SPEAKER_12]: It must be down low, I don't feel it.

764
01:03:10,306 --> 01:03:13,648
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, a lot of capital needs.

765
01:03:14,008 --> 01:03:16,130
[SPEAKER_12]: I think that's the long story short.

766
01:03:18,340 --> 01:03:19,561
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm not telling you anything you don't know.

767
01:03:20,162 --> 01:03:23,945
[SPEAKER_08]: Medford's the 20th largest municipality in Massachusetts.

768
01:03:24,005 --> 01:03:24,905
[SPEAKER_08]: We sometimes forget that.

769
01:03:27,959 --> 01:03:29,359
[SPEAKER_08]: those have grown up here and live here.

770
01:03:29,499 --> 01:03:33,120
[SPEAKER_08]: We look at this as like a nice bedroom community that we treat different, but we are a large city.

771
01:03:33,480 --> 01:03:38,561
[SPEAKER_08]: We have large city problems and there are large city demands and that includes on funding and everything else that comes with it, right?

772
01:03:38,981 --> 01:03:45,783
[SPEAKER_08]: But if with right systems in place, you overcome those troubles and you move forward and you correct them when you can correct them.

773
01:03:46,023 --> 01:03:48,324
[SPEAKER_08]: So I always like to kind of point that out.

774
01:03:48,904 --> 01:03:54,325
[SPEAKER_08]: I try to remind people that we are a large city police department with large city issues that we have to deal with.

775
01:03:55,705 --> 01:03:57,366
[SPEAKER_08]: And I don't know why it sometimes gets lost.

776
01:03:57,446 --> 01:03:59,107
[SPEAKER_08]: And I think it's just the feeling around here.

777
01:03:59,147 --> 01:04:01,708
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a, Medford's got a unique feel and a unique community.

778
01:04:01,728 --> 01:04:02,648
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a great community, right?

779
01:04:03,069 --> 01:04:08,311
[SPEAKER_08]: And we feel close-knit and tight and sometimes like a town, large city, right?

780
01:04:08,371 --> 01:04:09,952
[SPEAKER_08]: And we rank up there, so.

781
01:04:10,872 --> 01:04:11,432
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Chief.

782
01:04:11,472 --> 01:04:13,013
[SPEAKER_12]: A couple more questions.

783
01:04:13,994 --> 01:04:15,814
[SPEAKER_12]: Just a clarification around staffing.

784
01:04:16,915 --> 01:04:21,897
[SPEAKER_12]: So you said it's the, that minus 10 is a reduction in vacant positions, but not active layoffs.

785
01:04:22,357 --> 01:04:22,617
[SPEAKER_08]: Correct.

786
01:04:24,847 --> 01:04:33,214
[SPEAKER_12]: At that level of 73, how many positions, or even across the whole 102, how many vacant positions are there?

787
01:04:34,896 --> 01:04:40,821
[SPEAKER_08]: We have 102 active police officers, counting the seven that are coming out of the police academy Friday.

788
01:04:41,301 --> 01:04:43,703
[SPEAKER_12]: So there'll be no vacant positions as of Friday?

789
01:04:44,864 --> 01:04:47,206
[SPEAKER_08]: Based on that, yes, there will still be the 10 unfunded.

790
01:04:47,506 --> 01:04:49,188
[SPEAKER_12]: Yes, yes, yes, just unfunded.

791
01:04:50,008 --> 01:04:51,209
[SPEAKER_12]: Okay, that's helpful.

792
01:04:54,092 --> 01:04:55,813
[SPEAKER_12]: Do you foresee any impact?

793
01:04:55,933 --> 01:05:04,379
[SPEAKER_12]: I know this has been an issue on construction details of timing and staffing with the, going from the 83 to 73 patrol officers.

794
01:05:07,921 --> 01:05:09,562
[SPEAKER_08]: So the answer would be yes, right?

795
01:05:09,662 --> 01:05:17,067
[SPEAKER_08]: And, but we, I think part of that is not just a staff and part of it's just a historical change, right?

796
01:05:17,347 --> 01:05:22,270
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, we have a lot of times in front of where this,

797
01:05:23,725 --> 01:05:30,155
[SPEAKER_08]: We have a younger generation of police officers who are focusing on their careers and their jobs, and they're not as active as other police officers when I come out.

798
01:05:31,085 --> 01:05:35,186
[SPEAKER_08]: desperate to buy a house and do all these sort of things to get this thing done and you're more active in the work a lot.

799
01:05:35,246 --> 01:05:38,087
[SPEAKER_08]: We don't have as many personnel currently who work there who want to work.

800
01:05:38,807 --> 01:05:46,430
[SPEAKER_08]: And so that's, it's one of our challenges on a daily basis is to get, you know, other, our officers to work a little bit more in and of themselves.

801
01:05:46,670 --> 01:05:53,412
[SPEAKER_08]: So certainly though, coupled with that, if you lose 10 officers or you don't fund 10 offices, that's 10 additional offices you may lose for that.

802
01:05:54,152 --> 01:05:56,453
[SPEAKER_08]: But last year we struggled, right?

803
01:05:56,493 --> 01:05:58,053
[SPEAKER_08]: There was a lot of construction.

804
01:05:58,613 --> 01:05:59,154
[SPEAKER_08]: in this city.

805
01:05:59,274 --> 01:06:02,157
[SPEAKER_08]: And sometimes one large construction site is not a problem.

806
01:06:02,617 --> 01:06:08,262
[SPEAKER_08]: It's when you have a construction site, a construction site, a construction site, and they impact each other, you're scrambling for extra bodies, and that's it.

807
01:06:08,322 --> 01:06:09,283
[SPEAKER_08]: This year, we don't have that.

808
01:06:09,623 --> 01:06:17,070
[SPEAKER_08]: We've worked with Eversource pretty carefully right now to try to get them out of the city and to kind of, you know, work with us and cooperate.

809
01:06:17,370 --> 01:06:18,731
[SPEAKER_08]: And this year, that's how it's working.

810
01:06:18,771 --> 01:06:23,716
[SPEAKER_08]: And so we're filling our construction details right now.

811
01:06:24,256 --> 01:06:24,717
[SPEAKER_12]: All right, great.

812
01:06:25,997 --> 01:06:30,359
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, wouldn't it be great 10 was it seven eight years now ever source has been with us.

813
01:06:32,240 --> 01:06:38,022
[SPEAKER_12]: You mentioned a little bit the post stuff and the you know compliance with the state laws.

814
01:06:39,023 --> 01:06:47,987
[SPEAKER_12]: Just wanted to you know, just want to clarify, you said that you know it's a lot of work it's taken a lot of time, but, but you feel like you're able to keep up with it or.

815
01:06:48,953 --> 01:06:50,213
[SPEAKER_08]: So we're keeping up with it, right?

816
01:06:50,233 --> 01:06:51,213
[SPEAKER_08]: We're almost done.

817
01:06:51,634 --> 01:06:53,214
[SPEAKER_08]: So it's going to be every year from now on, right?

818
01:06:53,274 --> 01:07:03,616
[SPEAKER_08]: But the idea, I guess, is this is the first year, and they're doing A through H. And next year, they're going to do, that's brand new for all those offices and brand new for the chief of police.

819
01:07:03,696 --> 01:07:08,657
[SPEAKER_08]: And next year after, it'll be I through, say, P, and that'll be brand new for those offices.

820
01:07:09,097 --> 01:07:12,798
[SPEAKER_08]: But in three years time, it's just going to be repeat for the new offices.

821
01:07:12,858 --> 01:07:14,838
[SPEAKER_08]: And systems will be done, and systems will be in place.

822
01:07:15,378 --> 01:07:26,665
[SPEAKER_08]: The difficulty we're having is getting clarification from the post commission so that, and I don't necessarily always blame them, the legislature, just drafted this huge police reform packet, and they did so.

823
01:07:26,686 --> 01:07:32,609
[SPEAKER_08]: And I'll say this, you know, freely from my position with a lot of it without a lot of input from police.

824
01:07:33,690 --> 01:07:36,372
[SPEAKER_08]: One clear example is they just randomly picked letters a to H.

825
01:07:36,970 --> 01:07:41,693
[SPEAKER_08]: Turns out HOH, it's like 10, 11,000 police officers in Massachusetts.

826
01:07:41,713 --> 01:07:42,673
[SPEAKER_08]: It's a vast majority.

827
01:07:42,713 --> 01:07:46,255
[SPEAKER_08]: They thought they were dividing it up equally, but we could have told them that that's not it.

828
01:07:46,275 --> 01:07:49,116
[SPEAKER_08]: So little things like that are really causing a lot of problems.

829
01:07:49,497 --> 01:08:02,464
[SPEAKER_08]: The standards, though, that we're being able to ask and judge and compare police officers, I will tell you without getting into it, on July 13th, the Post Commission addressed the Massachusetts Police Commission and delivered a message on June 14th to the chiefs of police that we got.

830
01:08:02,544 --> 01:08:04,645
[SPEAKER_08]: And we all looked at it and said, this is due tomorrow.

831
01:08:05,585 --> 01:08:11,310
[SPEAKER_08]: So little things like that are constantly changing, make us go back and redo work and make us reevaluate the things that we're doing.

832
01:08:13,432 --> 01:08:19,118
[SPEAKER_08]: Having said all this, look, the theory of police reform and the theory of post commission and standardized police training and all this sort of stuff.

833
01:08:19,538 --> 01:08:20,959
[SPEAKER_08]: I am a strong proponent of it.

834
01:08:21,520 --> 01:08:25,564
[SPEAKER_08]: I am not a proponent of the cluster that they have created to try to get there.

835
01:08:25,904 --> 01:08:27,005
[SPEAKER_08]: And they refuse to sort of

836
01:08:27,527 --> 01:08:30,488
[SPEAKER_08]: But the legislation says we have to do this and make issues go forward.

837
01:08:30,748 --> 01:08:34,050
[SPEAKER_08]: It is a nightmare for police departments and for police chiefs trying to figure this out.

838
01:08:34,710 --> 01:08:43,534
[SPEAKER_08]: And so I mean, well, I won't get into the hours that you put into it, but it's, we'll get there eventually, right?

839
01:08:43,634 --> 01:08:45,155
[SPEAKER_08]: And get it, but it is a lot of work.

840
01:08:45,855 --> 01:08:56,840
[SPEAKER_12]: If there's one thing this council can sympathize with, at least in the past few weeks, it's floating deadlines and changing directives and not knowing exactly the information that we wish that we had.

841
01:08:57,470 --> 01:08:59,532
[SPEAKER_12]: So I personally can sympathize with that.

842
01:08:59,552 --> 01:09:02,314
[SPEAKER_12]: I know you've been dealing with it for a lot longer than the past few weeks.

843
01:09:03,715 --> 01:09:07,418
[SPEAKER_12]: I noticed in the report that professional standards commander position was vacant.

844
01:09:07,438 --> 01:09:08,119
[SPEAKER_12]: Has that been filled?

845
01:09:08,559 --> 01:09:09,960
[SPEAKER_08]: So it's in the process of being filled.

846
01:09:10,020 --> 01:09:11,842
[SPEAKER_08]: So you asked me, are there any unfilled positions?

847
01:09:12,262 --> 01:09:17,947
[SPEAKER_08]: I count that being filled, even though we're assessing that new captain now.

848
01:09:18,447 --> 01:09:25,373
[SPEAKER_08]: And so that position will be filled and it still gives us the 73 police officers underneath it.

849
01:09:25,678 --> 01:09:32,563
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, and then the last thing is, I'll just say is, you know, I think I'm not on the subcommittee, obviously, but I've been watching some of the meetings.

850
01:09:33,183 --> 01:09:38,047
[SPEAKER_12]: And, you know, I think there's an ongoing conversation around surveillance technology, and I appreciate the engagement with that.

851
01:09:38,067 --> 01:09:43,351
[SPEAKER_12]: And I'm hoping we can find a, you know, not to speak for the members subcommittee, of course.

852
01:09:43,971 --> 01:09:48,755
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, I'm personally just looking forward to how those conversations play out and hopefully finding a

853
01:09:49,529 --> 01:09:56,591
[SPEAKER_12]: an approach that works for everyone involved without putting too much more on the plate of the administration of your department.

854
01:09:56,931 --> 01:09:59,252
[SPEAKER_08]: Well, I will tell you I'm never shy to have a conversation.

855
01:09:59,532 --> 01:10:02,393
[SPEAKER_08]: And you can see how long sometimes some of these conversations last with me.

856
01:10:02,453 --> 01:10:15,257
[SPEAKER_08]: But I mean, I just always express, especially now this upcoming year, me losing 10 bodies doesn't mean that I'm going to do 10 less, 400 hours less hours of work a week, right?

857
01:10:15,717 --> 01:10:16,597
[SPEAKER_08]: I have to replace that.

858
01:10:18,458 --> 01:10:31,769
[SPEAKER_08]: I hesitate to use the term man I was but I think everyone knows what I'm talking about this there's a lot of things that have to be diverted to that and bringing in new systems is something that, you know, I'm going to be really cautious with because there's commitments that I'm willing to make if I can do them and if I can't, I can't.

859
01:10:32,049 --> 01:10:35,131
[SPEAKER_08]: That being said, I think conversation is has been.

860
01:10:35,952 --> 01:10:37,393
[SPEAKER_08]: better than I expected, right?

861
01:10:37,413 --> 01:10:45,518
[SPEAKER_08]: You know, sometimes there's a fear that somebody's in there just to put a stranglehold on the police department, and there's been a lot of reasonableness, and I think we can eventually come to some terms.

862
01:10:45,558 --> 01:10:49,821
[SPEAKER_08]: And so, you know, Council Collins has really been involved in that.

863
01:10:49,841 --> 01:10:51,082
[SPEAKER_08]: We have a lot of good conversations.

864
01:10:51,102 --> 01:10:52,042
[SPEAKER_08]: So, when not

865
01:10:54,321 --> 01:10:57,144
[SPEAKER_08]: It's not a fear here, but it's just a management type situation.

866
01:10:57,184 --> 01:11:02,027
[SPEAKER_08]: Like I can't, like, I will always say to you, I can't commit to something if I can't do it.

