01:45:51,723 --> 01:45:52,704
[SPEAKER_12]: I had a few questions.

1303
01:45:54,306 --> 01:46:07,041
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, this Council has made it very clearly a priority to improve and, you know, essentially single-handedly to improve the governance of the Elections Department, to modernize the department, to bring it into

1304
01:46:08,037 --> 01:46:19,193
[SPEAKER_12]: the 21st century, to staff it properly, and to make sure that there is, you know, a fully staffed modern elections department in the city of Medford that's getting everything right.

1305
01:46:19,853 --> 01:46:20,735
[SPEAKER_12]: And, you know,

1306
01:46:21,612 --> 01:46:29,217
[SPEAKER_12]: And then I don't mean everything right on election day, that's been the one thing that's been going well, it's everything right the other 363 days a year.

1307
01:46:29,277 --> 01:46:37,482
[SPEAKER_12]: And now that's changed too with early voting and mail voting, there's an election months before an election instead of election days.

1308
01:46:38,623 --> 01:46:49,689
[SPEAKER_12]: One thing that really perturbs me here and disturbs me quite frankly here is that this budget's being reduced after a year in which it was significantly increased because the,

1309
01:46:50,873 --> 01:46:55,295
[SPEAKER_12]: maintenance and operations of elections is so much more difficult now than it used to be.

1310
01:46:56,576 --> 01:46:59,577
[SPEAKER_12]: Just today, the Votes Act became law.

1311
01:46:59,777 --> 01:47:00,358
[SPEAKER_10]: That's correct.

1312
01:47:00,438 --> 01:47:02,759
[SPEAKER_12]: Permanent mail-in voting is now the law.

1313
01:47:03,339 --> 01:47:05,240
[SPEAKER_12]: Extended early voting is now the law.

1314
01:47:05,380 --> 01:47:05,720
[SPEAKER_03]: Correct.

1315
01:47:06,080 --> 01:47:10,082
[SPEAKER_12]: Now down to, I think, 10 days before the election, you can register, and from 20.

1316
01:47:10,222 --> 01:47:16,706
[SPEAKER_12]: I mean, the needs and the demands on an elections department by state law just grew immensely.

1317
01:47:17,166 --> 01:47:19,347
[SPEAKER_12]: But instead, in this budget, we're cutting.

1318
01:47:20,277 --> 01:47:21,718
[SPEAKER_12]: the budget for elections.

1319
01:47:22,818 --> 01:47:26,781
[SPEAKER_12]: And I'll just, you know, I don't think I need to remind anyone, this is a democracy, right?

1320
01:47:26,801 --> 01:47:28,041
[SPEAKER_12]: This is a democratic government.

1321
01:47:28,502 --> 01:47:37,247
[SPEAKER_12]: I was elected, you know, the mayor was elected, the school committee was elected, my fellow counselors were elected, and this is the department that manages the process by which we are elected.

1322
01:47:37,267 --> 01:47:45,091
[SPEAKER_12]: Nevermind the fact that we also elect state representatives and state senators and federal officials, and we're about to elect a new governor.

1323
01:47:47,262 --> 01:47:53,728
[SPEAKER_12]: And from what I can tell, we're cutting the head count when the workload's going up.

1324
01:47:54,448 --> 01:47:56,330
[SPEAKER_12]: And that just is really worrying to me.

1325
01:47:56,590 --> 01:48:04,477
[SPEAKER_12]: And I know that it's quote unquote difficult times and quote unquote, we can't find anything anywhere.

1326
01:48:05,217 --> 01:48:07,720
[SPEAKER_12]: We're not talking hundreds of thousands or millions of dollars.

1327
01:48:08,180 --> 01:48:13,925
[SPEAKER_12]: We're talking in the tens of thousands of dollars here to have the adequate funding that we need for elections.

1328
01:48:15,087 --> 01:48:18,030
[SPEAKER_12]: One of our council priorities really focused around elections as well.

1329
01:48:18,110 --> 01:48:19,711
