1562
02:12:39,890 --> 02:12:42,431
[SPEAKER_05]: Not in particular, just that it's a level funded board and committee.

1563
02:12:43,652 --> 02:12:45,333
[SPEAKER_12]: All right, any questions from the council?

1564
02:12:46,293 --> 02:12:48,574
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, any questions from the public?

1565
02:12:51,596 --> 02:12:53,577
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, we'll move to the Bicycle Commission.

1566
02:12:54,397 --> 02:12:55,377
[SPEAKER_12]: I will pull that one up.

1567
02:12:59,920 --> 02:13:03,561
[SPEAKER_12]: Bicycle Advisory Commission, $1,500, no change.

1568
02:13:04,282 --> 02:13:06,163
[SPEAKER_12]: Madam Chief of Staff, if you have any presentation.

1569
02:13:06,883 --> 02:13:08,904
[SPEAKER_05]: Same comments, level funded board and commission.

1570
02:13:09,948 --> 02:13:11,109
[SPEAKER_12]: Any questions from the council?

1571
02:13:11,129 --> 02:13:14,432
[SPEAKER_12]: Any questions from the public?

1572
02:13:16,433 --> 02:13:21,358
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, move to, let's go to insurance.

1573
02:13:22,138 --> 02:13:26,262
[SPEAKER_12]: That's another one that's only available digitally.

1574
02:13:26,362 --> 02:13:27,943
[SPEAKER_12]: So give me one moment to pull that up.

1575
02:13:28,804 --> 02:13:34,568
[SPEAKER_12]: This is one of the major expenses of the city currently proposed at 27,938,312.

1576
02:13:39,246 --> 02:13:41,087
[SPEAKER_12]: an increase of $1,488,651 over the prior year, or about 5.6%.

1577
02:13:41,127 --> 02:13:44,589
[SPEAKER_12]: I believe this is inclusive both of the, let me pull the spreadsheet breakdown, it is Medicare for the city and school,

1578
02:13:55,752 --> 02:13:59,994
[SPEAKER_12]: The largest expense is the health, life and dental insurance for our city and school employees.

1579
02:14:00,534 --> 02:14:09,317
[SPEAKER_12]: It's also accounts for unemployment insurance, certain amounts of fringe benefits as well as property insurance, insurance premiums and police liability.

1580
02:14:09,957 --> 02:14:11,918
[SPEAKER_12]: Any presentation, Madam Chief of Staff.

1581
02:14:13,499 --> 02:14:13,799
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

1582
02:14:13,839 --> 02:14:17,380
[SPEAKER_05]: I defer to Finance Director and Auditor Dickinson.

1583
02:14:18,697 --> 02:14:29,021
[SPEAKER_05]: By and large, all I really can say here is it's a budget that's always increasing, and we continue to evaluate cost opportunities and to reduce.

1584
02:14:29,041 --> 02:14:29,802
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

1585
02:14:39,765 --> 02:14:46,248
[SPEAKER_02]: Essentially, ditto that, we make a projection based on trends

1586
02:14:47,907 --> 02:14:48,847
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where we came in at.

1587
02:14:49,288 --> 02:14:51,689
[SPEAKER_02]: So I have to pay it.

1588
02:14:53,009 --> 02:14:55,350
[SPEAKER_12]: Any questions from members of the city council?

1589
02:14:56,051 --> 02:14:57,211
[SPEAKER_12]: I have two questions.

1590
02:14:57,691 --> 02:15:00,432
[SPEAKER_12]: One just, do we ever come in under budget on this one?

1591
02:15:00,452 --> 02:15:02,994
[SPEAKER_02]: I do not know that.

1592
02:15:03,454 --> 02:15:03,734
[SPEAKER_12]: Okay.

1593
02:15:04,734 --> 02:15:09,756
[SPEAKER_12]: Is it possible based on the projections that costs could be lower than the expected 5.6% increase?

1594
02:15:11,337 --> 02:15:12,178
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always possible.

1595
02:15:12,198 --> 02:15:12,978
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be nice.

1596
02:15:14,237 --> 02:15:27,182
[SPEAKER_12]: And this is less a question more a point when a 27.9 I think if I got that right million dollar budget is increasing by five or 6% a year.

1597
02:15:27,702 --> 02:15:30,624
[SPEAKER_12]: And the revenues of the city are constricted to two and a half percent a year.

1598
02:15:31,124 --> 02:15:33,885
[SPEAKER_12]: That's exactly what we're talking about when we're talking about how

1599
02:15:34,974 --> 02:15:37,597
[SPEAKER_12]: cost growths are outpacing our ability to raise revenue.

1600
02:15:38,779 --> 02:15:52,715
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, this is a significant expense, it's a high growth expense I think anyone who has health insurance for any capacity understands how expensive it is whether it's private insurance or, you know, through your employer or I was on the individual market for a couple years that was no fun.

1601
02:15:55,643 --> 02:16:02,049
[SPEAKER_12]: Again, I think it just drives home the point that our costs are outpacing our revenues and the city needs to do something about that.

1602
02:16:02,229 --> 02:16:04,771
[SPEAKER_12]: There are limited options before us to do something about that.

1603
02:16:05,371 --> 02:16:12,758
[SPEAKER_12]: And if we don't do something about that, these costs growths will crowd out important investments that we need to make in our city and our schools.

1604
02:16:13,592 --> 02:16:14,352
[SPEAKER_12]: Councilor Kerrio.

1605
02:16:14,713 --> 02:16:19,675
[SPEAKER_06]: Mr. President, do we know when the last time we went off the bid on our insurance?

1606
02:16:19,775 --> 02:16:22,256
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't know when the last time we did that.

1607
02:16:22,836 --> 02:16:23,496
[SPEAKER_12]: Which insurance?

1608
02:16:23,817 --> 02:16:24,937
[SPEAKER_06]: On the insurance costs.

1609
02:16:25,897 --> 02:16:26,858
[SPEAKER_12]: For the health insurance?

1610
02:16:27,038 --> 02:16:28,178
[SPEAKER_12]: I believe that's through the GIC.

1611
02:16:28,218 --> 02:16:29,579
[SPEAKER_12]: I'm not sure how the process works.

1612
02:16:30,019 --> 02:16:31,480
[SPEAKER_06]: It's through the GIC.

1613
02:16:32,260 --> 02:16:35,502
[SPEAKER_06]: I know that's coming due soon, coming to an end.

1614
02:16:36,482 --> 02:16:36,822
[SPEAKER_12]: I don't know.

1615
02:16:37,403 --> 02:16:41,584
[SPEAKER_12]: Could we describe what the process is for how we determine our health insurance costs potentially?

1616
02:16:44,812 --> 02:16:48,153
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not aware, I don't know that right now.

1617
02:16:48,173 --> 02:16:50,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Madam Chair and staff, I see your hand raised.

1618
02:16:50,414 --> 02:16:51,335
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, yes.

1619
02:16:51,415 --> 02:16:55,276
[SPEAKER_05]: Vice President Behrs, to you and city councilors, Councilor Caraviello.

1620
02:16:57,057 --> 02:17:07,781
[SPEAKER_05]: So the process that the city of Medford has in place is governed by statute because the city accepted a public employee commission under section 19 of I believe chapter 32.

1621
02:17:07,901 --> 02:17:13,223
[SPEAKER_05]: I'd have to double check the chapter, but there are very strict

1622
02:17:14,003 --> 02:17:19,068
[SPEAKER_05]: procedures in which the city has to follow in order to consider alternatives.

1623
02:17:19,789 --> 02:17:33,163
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's my understanding that historically that the city went to the GIC and adopted this plan given rates and actual losses that were impacting the city's costs.

1624
02:17:34,044 --> 02:17:35,105
[SPEAKER_05]: Once in the GIC,

1625
02:17:35,846 --> 02:17:40,209
[SPEAKER_05]: In order to consider alternative options, there is a lengthy procedure for that.

1626
02:17:40,249 --> 02:17:48,295
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, you can actually evaluate it, but by and large, it's reasonably known by industry standards that you're not going to get better rates.

1627
02:17:48,795 --> 02:17:58,963
[SPEAKER_05]: The other advantage to the GIC is that it offers many different plans so that employees can pick a plan that is appropriate for them and their families.

1628
02:18:00,640 --> 02:18:11,690
[SPEAKER_05]: In any event, as for specifics on further details relative to GIC, I don't know the specific last time that the rates were evaluated.

1629
02:18:12,010 --> 02:18:21,939
[SPEAKER_05]: But again, the city is locked into the GIC unless it puts the GIC on notification by a certain date in December that it wishes to consider removing itself.

1630
02:18:23,561 --> 02:18:27,445
[SPEAKER_05]: The city is presently in negotiations with the public employee committee.

1631
02:18:27,745 --> 02:18:29,527
[SPEAKER_05]: Those negotiations began recently.

1632
02:18:30,048 --> 02:18:31,930
[SPEAKER_05]: And so there may be updates in the future.

1633
02:18:33,151 --> 02:18:41,079
[SPEAKER_05]: And furthermore, some of the ways that the city can potentially address cost increases is plan changes.

1634
02:18:41,606 --> 02:18:53,930
[SPEAKER_05]: Those plan changes would be probably undesirable to employees, but those are the kinds of things that have to be considered throughout the process to evaluate costs pertaining to healthcare.

1635
02:18:56,091 --> 02:18:56,571
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you.

1636
02:18:56,852 --> 02:18:58,292
[SPEAKER_12]: Any further questions on insurance?

1637
02:18:59,833 --> 02:19:03,374
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, any further questions from the public on insurance?

1638
02:19:06,753 --> 02:19:09,374
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, we'll move on to workers' compensation.

1639
02:19:09,394 --> 02:19:11,095
[SPEAKER_12]: This is also digital only.

1640
02:19:11,115 --> 02:19:22,079
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, well, there was, I think, a miscommunication by myself with the chief of staff.

1641
02:19:22,500 --> 02:19:31,183
[SPEAKER_12]: I thought we were going to be getting physical budget books on Tuesday, and then I was made aware that we were only gonna be getting digital budget books, so I said we didn't need the budget sheets for these departments.

1642
02:19:34,284 --> 02:19:35,665
[SPEAKER_12]: That's why we are where we are with that.

1643
02:19:37,547 --> 02:19:45,169
[SPEAKER_12]: It is available online, although it's kind of difficult to... It's not as easy as having a book.

1644
02:19:47,009 --> 02:19:49,210
[SPEAKER_12]: Workers' compensation, expenditure summary $646,000.

1645
02:19:49,390 --> 02:19:51,671
[SPEAKER_12]: That's a reduction of $106,500 from the prior year, or 14.15%.

1646
02:19:51,711 --> 02:19:55,191
[SPEAKER_12]: Any presentation from Director Dickinson or Chief of Staff Nazarian regarding workers' compensation?

1647
02:20:06,265 --> 02:20:20,311
[SPEAKER_02]: The reduction is simply due to there were some settlements in the prior fiscal year, so we have some coming up, so assume that that's where this number comes from, essentially.

1648
02:20:20,331 --> 02:20:29,755
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just historical data with a little bit more added in, but it's not as much as it was last year because, again, you've settled some stuff.

1649
02:20:30,295 --> 02:20:31,036
[SPEAKER_12]: Okay, thank you.

1650
02:20:31,076 --> 02:20:32,496
[SPEAKER_12]: That answers my only question.

1651
02:20:32,536 --> 02:20:33,637
[SPEAKER_12]: Any questions from the Council?

1652
02:20:35,276 --> 02:20:37,497
[SPEAKER_12]: Any questions from the public or comments from the public?

1653
02:20:39,639 --> 02:20:41,460
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, we'll move to pensions.

1654
02:20:42,100 --> 02:20:42,701
[SPEAKER_12]: Pensions.

1655
02:20:52,787 --> 02:20:55,009
[SPEAKER_12]: We don't have any further budget meetings scheduled.

1656
02:20:58,711 --> 02:21:03,154
[SPEAKER_12]: We can table any questions till discussion at our next regular meeting when we consider the budget.

1657
02:21:06,470 --> 02:21:14,856
[SPEAKER_06]: is that I will I was going to ask table in the table, the debt service and bonds and interest too.

1658
02:21:14,936 --> 02:21:17,018
[SPEAKER_06]: And so we had until we had the rest of the members here.

1659
02:21:18,839 --> 02:21:28,106
[SPEAKER_06]: Because I think those are pretty important departments that, you know, unfortunately, you know, we have some members missing tonight.

1660
02:21:30,188 --> 02:21:34,451
[SPEAKER_06]: Something that we could just discuss before the before for the council meeting, maybe on Tuesday.

1661
02:21:36,595 --> 02:21:42,098
[SPEAKER_06]: if the president would like to schedule that.

1662
02:21:42,138 --> 02:21:44,799
[SPEAKER_12]: Are there any other items that you'd like to say?

1663
02:21:44,839 --> 02:21:52,002
[SPEAKER_06]: I think the debt service and the bonds and interest of the pensions, we're looking at many millions of dollars.

1664
02:21:52,842 --> 02:21:53,923
[SPEAKER_06]: I think that needs a little more.

1665
02:21:54,523 --> 02:21:57,764
[SPEAKER_06]: I think the other council should have the opportunity to ask questions on.

1666
02:21:59,085 --> 02:22:02,006
[SPEAKER_06]: So if it'd be the pleasure of the president

1667
02:22:03,608 --> 02:22:11,771
[SPEAKER_06]: the whole, maybe a brief committee of the whole meeting on Tuesday before the council for those couple of things, I think that would be helpful.

1668
02:22:11,971 --> 02:22:13,952
[SPEAKER_12]: Do you think 6.15 PM would be sufficient?

1669
02:22:13,972 --> 02:22:16,273
[SPEAKER_06]: 6.15 should be enough for those two.

1670
02:22:16,513 --> 02:22:19,454
[SPEAKER_12]: I will coordinate with President Morrell to see if we can schedule that.

1671
02:22:19,654 --> 02:22:26,416
[SPEAKER_12]: On the motion of Councilor, Councilor Kerrio to the table, pensions seconded by.

1672
02:22:27,017 --> 02:22:29,297
[SPEAKER_06]: Pensions and debt service and bonds and interest.

1673
02:22:31,372 --> 02:22:32,672
[SPEAKER_12]: Seconded by Councilor Sang.

1674
02:22:32,692 --> 02:22:33,512
[SPEAKER_12]: All those in favor?

1675
02:22:33,532 --> 02:22:33,812
[SPEAKER_12]: Aye.

1676
02:22:34,253 --> 02:22:34,673
[SPEAKER_12]: Opposed?

1677
02:22:35,473 --> 02:22:36,333
[SPEAKER_12]: Motion passes.

1678
02:22:45,695 --> 02:22:54,776
[SPEAKER_12]: That leaves us with Information Technology, which was previously tabled, Legislative, which was previously tabled, and the Executive, which was before us tonight.

1679
02:22:54,796 --> 02:22:58,617
[SPEAKER_12]: Do we want to take Information Technology first?

1680
02:23:02,966 --> 02:23:05,668
[SPEAKER_12]: Information technology is previously tabled.

1681
02:23:05,768 --> 02:23:18,977
[SPEAKER_12]: We did receive a redacted report, and I believe there may be a request from the council to meet in some form of executive session to discuss unredacted information regarding a cybersecurity analysis that was done in the past year.

1682
02:23:20,198 --> 02:23:26,862
[SPEAKER_12]: Just with that information, I wanted to let people know why it was tabled, and I'm happy to turn it over to Chief of Staff Nazarian for a presentation.

1683
02:23:31,799 --> 02:23:33,459
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Vice President Behrs.

1684
02:23:34,600 --> 02:23:41,661
[SPEAKER_05]: So the Information Technology Department budget is by and large pretty much the same as last year.

1685
02:23:42,181 --> 02:23:46,142
[SPEAKER_05]: We are posting the position actually.

1686
02:23:46,182 --> 02:23:52,243
[SPEAKER_05]: We're actively working to finalize that with a number of different support folks.

1687
02:23:52,283 --> 02:23:56,684
[SPEAKER_05]: We have someone in the community that's helping who has specific knowledge in this area.

1688
02:23:57,589 --> 02:24:10,777
[SPEAKER_05]: It's important in particular to evaluate that job because historically the information technology department appears to have kind of evolved in a way that has not been centralized.

1689
02:24:11,258 --> 02:24:24,145
[SPEAKER_05]: We have, for instance, phone systems that don't necessarily fall under the IT department, but you have to go to one department to get the phone and another department to actually

1690
02:24:25,653 --> 02:24:31,092
[SPEAKER_05]: get, you know, the extension or some of these other details which, which it's important to centralize these things so it's

1691
02:24:31,523 --> 02:24:37,806
[SPEAKER_05]: It's critical for us to evaluate that job description to make sure it accurately reflects the goals and the mission of the department.

1692
02:24:38,586 --> 02:24:51,612
[SPEAKER_05]: And I hope that the counselors and the public will look at the description that's provided in the budget book, because I think it really provides a good understanding of where we're trying to take the information technology department in general.

1693
02:24:52,432 --> 02:24:58,795
[SPEAKER_05]: A number of different things that we're working on over the course of the next, probably several years, because it's gonna take time.

1694
02:25:00,128 --> 02:25:12,592
[SPEAKER_05]: is developing a list of assets, both software and hardware, developing a process, specific policies and procedures for information technology and streamlining.

1695
02:25:12,992 --> 02:25:20,095
[SPEAKER_05]: We've presently developed a ticketing system that we're utilizing a third party vendor to provide us support on that.

1696
02:25:20,215 --> 02:25:27,457
[SPEAKER_05]: And actually that third party vendor is presently providing a service support as well and managed services type of environment.

1697
02:25:29,983 --> 02:25:39,067
[SPEAKER_05]: By and large, I think that concludes my presentation, but I'm happy to answer any questions that the city council may have.

1698
02:25:39,647 --> 02:25:42,688
[SPEAKER_12]: Chief or staff, any questions on information technology?

1699
02:25:48,450 --> 02:25:49,510
[SPEAKER_12]: Councillor Collins, is that?

1700
02:25:50,010 --> 02:25:50,191
[SPEAKER_12]: No.

1701
02:25:50,991 --> 02:25:51,831
[SPEAKER_12]: I have one question.

1702
02:25:53,272 --> 02:25:53,852
[SPEAKER_12]: Given the

1703
02:25:56,059 --> 02:26:10,090
[SPEAKER_12]: cybersecurity assessment and report, was there any, basically, was there any thought that this budget should be amended relative to the concerns outlined in that report, or was the thought that the amendments shouldn't be made until a director is hired?

1704
02:26:13,072 --> 02:26:14,753
[SPEAKER_05]: Vice President Bares, thank you for the question.

1705
02:26:15,654 --> 02:26:23,080
[SPEAKER_05]: In general, I mean, I would say that this department, like many of our other departments, could use more resources.

1706
02:26:23,759 --> 02:26:34,567
[SPEAKER_05]: Um, but it's important to the mayor and myself that we are making sure that we are obviously producing a budget that is sustainable.

1707
02:26:34,927 --> 02:26:45,114
[SPEAKER_05]: And so in that process, we have considered all different options and it is important that a director be hired to consider and evaluate the next steps.

1708
02:26:45,454 --> 02:26:52,559
[SPEAKER_05]: But we do have a very solid process for cybersecurity at this point in time, as it is managed by a third party.

1709
02:26:54,049 --> 02:26:54,869
[SPEAKER_12]: Is that toss?

1710
02:26:55,570 --> 02:26:56,070
[SPEAKER_05]: It is, yeah.

1711
02:26:57,050 --> 02:26:57,490
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you.

1712
02:26:57,630 --> 02:26:59,431
[SPEAKER_12]: Any further questions on information technology?

1713
02:27:01,052 --> 02:27:03,553
[SPEAKER_12]: Any comments from the public on information technology?

1714
02:27:05,333 --> 02:27:08,134
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, we'll move to the legislative budget.

1715
02:27:12,236 --> 02:27:16,037
[SPEAKER_12]: I guess I'm the acting chair of the legislature.

1716
02:27:18,010 --> 02:27:24,275
[SPEAKER_12]: So I will just go through and say that largely there is little change to the budget.

1717
02:27:24,995 --> 02:27:29,579
[SPEAKER_12]: There's a reduction of $900 to the part-time employees line item, but other than that, there was no change.

1718
02:27:32,161 --> 02:27:35,884
[SPEAKER_12]: This council did have some requests and recommendations regarding the legislative budget.

1719
02:27:35,924 --> 02:27:42,409
[SPEAKER_12]: Of course, the appropriation $50,000 for phase two zoning consultant for us to continue our zoning work.

1720
02:27:44,584 --> 02:27:49,265
[SPEAKER_12]: and in alignment with any of the other plans that are coming out that was not included in this budget.

1721
02:27:51,486 --> 02:27:59,448
[SPEAKER_12]: And also, I'll bring this up in a moment, but I'll defer to my fellow councilors first, if they have any questions about the legislative budget.

1722
02:28:00,308 --> 02:28:00,928
[SPEAKER_12]: Councilor Kerrio.

1723
02:28:01,349 --> 02:28:10,571
[SPEAKER_06]: Mr. President, the only question is, I think the clerk said, we're currently not using that software in the other two rooms that we're paying for.

1724
02:28:12,291 --> 02:28:13,772
[SPEAKER_06]: Is that needed in the budget still?

1725
02:28:16,012 --> 02:28:19,794
[SPEAKER_12]: I will allow Chief Staff Nazarian to speak in a minute.

1726
02:28:21,374 --> 02:28:32,758
[SPEAKER_12]: I would say this, personally, there's a lot of communities that have a much more up-to-date digitized system for things like council papers, council records, things of that nature.

1727
02:28:33,278 --> 02:28:39,961
[SPEAKER_12]: Rather than not have it in the budget, I would like to see us maybe explore a new contract, repurpose it for something like that.

1728
02:28:40,981 --> 02:28:43,143
[SPEAKER_12]: But I will also go to Chief of Staff Nazarian.

1729
02:28:43,883 --> 02:28:49,967
[SPEAKER_06]: And I agree with you, the addition of the money for the zoning consultant, I think is key.

1730
02:28:49,987 --> 02:29:01,473
[SPEAKER_06]: I mean, you saw, you've got two projects already, hopefully in the pipeline, more than paid for the small investment that we made in that.

1731
02:29:01,593 --> 02:29:05,155
[SPEAKER_06]: So I support putting the money in.

1732
02:29:05,676 --> 02:29:07,517
[SPEAKER_06]: Again, I don't know what number it's going to be.

1733
02:29:08,485 --> 02:29:17,229
[SPEAKER_06]: Maybe 50 is not enough, maybe it's too much I don't know but I think that's something we should, but there should be some type of expenditure in there to keep that project going.

1734
02:29:17,829 --> 02:29:18,969
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Councilor Kerrio.

1735
02:29:18,989 --> 02:29:27,193
[SPEAKER_12]: Do you have any comment regarding the technical services such as, I mean, I think we're basically talking about the camera system that's in room 207.

1736
02:29:28,414 --> 02:29:32,256
[SPEAKER_12]: I think we've now, that's basically a deprecated system.

1737
02:29:32,296 --> 02:29:33,017
[SPEAKER_12]: We've moved on.

1738
02:29:33,097 --> 02:29:35,218
[SPEAKER_12]: We're in a new era of these hybrid meetings.

1739
02:29:35,318 --> 02:29:37,759
[SPEAKER_12]: Medford Community Media has set up different technologies.

1740
02:29:37,779 --> 02:29:47,645
[SPEAKER_12]: Do you know if it would be possible for us to potentially leave that contract and potentially explore some different uses for technical services with those funds?

1741
02:29:49,586 --> 02:29:51,067
[SPEAKER_05]: Vice President Behrs, thank you for the question.

1742
02:29:51,087 --> 02:29:55,590
[SPEAKER_05]: I am not entirely sure I have the background on this one at this point, but

1743
02:29:56,638 --> 02:30:00,622
[SPEAKER_05]: Is the reference to room 207 and room 201 at this point?

1744
02:30:01,743 --> 02:30:09,631
[SPEAKER_12]: I think it's, so it's room 207 and this actually predates my time on the council as well.

1745
02:30:09,651 --> 02:30:10,552
[SPEAKER_12]: Maybe there's room 201 as well.

1746
02:30:10,592 --> 02:30:16,258
[SPEAKER_12]: Yes, I think the cameras, the streaming technology that was installed in those rooms at least four or five years ago.

1747
02:30:17,264 --> 02:30:18,204
[SPEAKER_05]: I see, okay, thank you.

1748
02:30:18,244 --> 02:30:21,385
[SPEAKER_05]: And I know that there has been discussion, I'm not sure where it stemmed from.

1749
02:30:21,925 --> 02:30:37,169
[SPEAKER_05]: I know that there has been discussion and an effort, and you may have referenced this, Vice President Behrs, that Medford Community Media assist with getting some new technology into at least room 207, but I know that that discussion has further expanded into room 201.

1750
02:30:37,629 --> 02:30:41,290
[SPEAKER_05]: We have, as a part of the information technology process,

1751
02:30:41,814 --> 02:30:49,919
[SPEAKER_05]: We have developed an ad hoc group of department heads and that includes our current Medford community manager Kevin Harrington.

1752
02:30:50,479 --> 02:30:55,923
[SPEAKER_05]: And also, this is just kind of in line with comments made by planning.

1753
02:30:58,220 --> 02:31:04,444
[SPEAKER_05]: Development and Sustainability Director, Alicia Hunt, at her budget hearing for that department.

1754
02:31:05,045 --> 02:31:08,908
[SPEAKER_05]: Teresa DuPont is a part of those discussions, as well as, of course, Director Hunt.

1755
02:31:09,408 --> 02:31:15,372
[SPEAKER_05]: And they've been collaborating, in particular, Teresa DuPont with Kevin Harrington, our MCM manager.

1756
02:31:15,913 --> 02:31:17,294
[SPEAKER_05]: And there's a manager title right there.

1757
02:31:18,855 --> 02:31:26,080
[SPEAKER_05]: And also to discuss, of course, the opportunities and ways that we can modify those rooms and their technologies.

1758
02:31:28,287 --> 02:31:34,431
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, look at the baby, you know, repurpose that money to other technological means that this Council can use.

1759
02:31:34,571 --> 02:31:36,832
[SPEAKER_12]: The City Clerk, I believe, had something maybe to say.

1760
02:31:37,332 --> 02:31:39,033
[SPEAKER_12]: Mr. Clerk, I saw you unmuted yourself.

1761
02:31:42,147 --> 02:31:45,129
[SPEAKER_07]: That was an anticipatory unmuting.

1762
02:31:46,750 --> 02:31:55,456
[SPEAKER_07]: There is not a whole lot that I can add other than I know that when that software was put into place, there were a lot of kinks that had to get worked out.

1763
02:31:56,396 --> 02:31:58,338
[SPEAKER_07]: My recollection is that it wasn't operative.

1764
02:31:59,018 --> 02:32:00,179
[SPEAKER_07]: It was installed sometime in 2018.

1765
02:32:02,332 --> 02:32:10,998
[SPEAKER_07]: was not actually operative, we're using a meeting sometime, I believe early 2019, I'm talking about calendar years, not fiscal years.

1766
02:32:11,539 --> 02:32:19,685
[SPEAKER_07]: And then very soon into the pandemic, because of the pandemic, the council stopped meeting in that room.

1767
02:32:19,725 --> 02:32:25,269
[SPEAKER_07]: So I don't believe that technology has been used in room 207 since very early in the pandemic.

1768
02:32:26,453 --> 02:32:27,333
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Mr. Clerk.

1769
02:32:27,353 --> 02:32:42,340
[SPEAKER_12]: I think Madam Chief of Staff, I think to maybe tie all of these different threads together, where we are is, we had Mr. Harrington before us discussing the new technologies, more Zoom-based, the Joppa speakers, things like that.

1770
02:32:44,961 --> 02:32:48,523
[SPEAKER_12]: The camera technologies that we're in,

1771
02:32:49,460 --> 02:32:54,230
[SPEAKER_12]: Room 201 and 207 that were previously installed by the clerk's estimation sometime in 2018.

1772
02:32:54,691 --> 02:32:58,579
[SPEAKER_12]: Those came with a specific software package that I believe had a subscription cost.

1773
02:32:59,503 --> 02:33:06,848
[SPEAKER_12]: Mr. Harrington said that those cameras, the way that they're connected to everything, as well as the software itself is basically useless for the city.

1774
02:33:07,729 --> 02:33:19,538
[SPEAKER_12]: So I think that basically what this council is saying is we would love to initiate the process to no longer have that subscription as the cameras and technology are not useful.

1775
02:33:20,138 --> 02:33:37,421
[SPEAKER_12]: and then perhaps this council will approach the administration or procurement or however we need to do it to repurpose that subscription cost towards something more like a modern digital system for tracking papers before the council, something like that.

1776
02:33:37,541 --> 02:33:43,983
[SPEAKER_12]: So I'm not exactly sure the procedure here, quite frankly, but I think that's just something to let you know of.

1777
02:33:44,043 --> 02:33:45,103
[SPEAKER_12]: I don't know if maybe you could,

1778
02:33:46,203 --> 02:33:52,989
[SPEAKER_12]: notify whoever is paying the subscription that we can leave that service as it's no longer used.

1779
02:33:53,629 --> 02:34:15,707
[SPEAKER_12]: And then this council could have discussions along with the city clerk about a new system that we may appropriate these technical services funds for, or request an appropriation, or again, it's appropriated, so I'm not exactly sure how it would work, but could work collaboratively with the administration to let you know what we were thinking and make sure that it would be a functional approach.

1780
02:34:16,798 --> 02:34:20,183
[SPEAKER_05]: Great, I appreciate that summarization, Vice President Behrs, that's very helpful.

1781
02:34:20,444 --> 02:34:29,058
[SPEAKER_05]: And yes, I can definitely look into further where that subscription is paid out of and evaluation of whether we should continue to your point.

1782
02:34:30,420 --> 02:34:39,044
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you, Mr President, and I'm going to cover yellow, if we could put some type of stipend in there for the zoning consultant going forward.

1783
02:34:39,944 --> 02:34:43,606
[SPEAKER_06]: This way we know it is going to happen in the future.

1784
02:34:45,527 --> 02:34:47,028
[SPEAKER_06]: Some dollar amount to get it started.

1785
02:34:47,048 --> 02:34:51,630
[SPEAKER_06]: I think would be helpful with rather than have nothing in the budget at all for that.

1786
02:34:52,588 --> 02:34:54,929
[SPEAKER_06]: I think it's a key tool.

1787
02:34:55,950 --> 02:35:05,574
[SPEAKER_06]: You've seen what we did with it in the past, and I think it's something we should continue on as phase two, as we see people are starting to look.

1788
02:35:06,234 --> 02:35:10,736
[SPEAKER_06]: And again, the longer we wait to do that, the more difficult it's going to get.

1789
02:35:11,096 --> 02:35:18,479
[SPEAKER_06]: So if the administration could find some funding to at least put it in the budget as a starting point, it would be appreciated.

1790
02:35:19,139 --> 02:35:20,720
[SPEAKER_12]: Madam Chief of Staff, do you have a comment on that?

1791
02:35:21,715 --> 02:35:30,082
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't have a specific comment on that but do we know how much the, the software amount is that's being that was discussed a moment ago.

1792
02:35:31,023 --> 02:35:33,244
[SPEAKER_06]: But 25,000 26,000 I think it's, I see you.

1793
02:35:34,485 --> 02:35:36,167
[SPEAKER_12]: I don't know if there may be multiple.

1794
02:35:36,207 --> 02:35:38,048
[SPEAKER_12]: Yeah, I don't know.

1795
02:35:38,709 --> 02:35:48,717
[SPEAKER_12]: So I don't want to say that this software, software alone that we may, I'm not sure where the city's muni code is paid out of, for example.

1796
02:35:50,852 --> 02:35:56,175
[SPEAKER_12]: So is your point that maybe that could be at least for one- It's just an option, thank you.

1797
02:35:56,195 --> 02:35:57,976
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just an option that I was thinking of.

1798
02:35:58,016 --> 02:36:04,900
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, it's certainly the mayor's budget, but just given the dialogue a moment ago, it's something that we could potentially look at.

1799
02:36:06,180 --> 02:36:07,321
[SPEAKER_12]: Okay, thank you.

1800
02:36:09,242 --> 02:36:11,643
[SPEAKER_12]: Councilor Kerrio, was that a motion or just- Yes.

1801
02:36:11,944 --> 02:36:15,646
[SPEAKER_12]: Okay, so on the motion of Councilor Kerrio to request that the administration

1802
02:36:16,507 --> 02:36:21,940
[SPEAKER_12]: provide some funding for zoning consultant in the fiscal 23 budget seconded by Councillor Collins.

1803
02:36:23,122 --> 02:36:24,145
[SPEAKER_12]: Mr. Clerk, do you have the motion.

1804
02:36:26,526 --> 02:36:39,355
[SPEAKER_11]: Any discussion counselor saying, I was just going to say you know this council is very excited to work with the mayor's administration on the comprehensive plan on the new projects that we have coming out of the pipeline OPS.

1805
02:36:39,515 --> 02:36:54,885
[SPEAKER_11]: You know, these are, you know, if we are excited to move forward in these terms and we're excited to work on increasing housing increasing commercial development in the city, but you know if we want to make sure that we do zone for it, we need the zoning consultant to make sure that

1806
02:36:55,801 --> 02:37:00,594
[SPEAKER_11]: you know, these plans are coming to fruition and aren't just, you know, stuck in the pipeline.

1807
02:37:01,476 --> 02:37:02,539
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Councilor Sang.

1808
02:37:02,559 --> 02:37:04,303
[SPEAKER_12]: Councilor Collins.

1809
02:37:06,125 --> 02:37:07,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Vice President Burris.

1810
02:37:07,986 --> 02:37:26,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to add my voice to the chorus there I think that Councilor Cabrillo said it very well when he said, you know, these, the, the types of projects that we've been able to pave the way for through the first phase of zoning recertification have paid for themselves or will pay for themselves so just to shore up that point for me.

1811
02:37:29,645 --> 02:37:41,209
[SPEAKER_01]: The chance for this Council to work with the zoning consultant to get on phase two is is really and I think I've probably made this clear before it's really a bright line for me in this budget, and I've said that before but I just want to be really transparent about that.

1812
02:37:43,287 --> 02:37:56,689
[SPEAKER_01]: desperately wanted to see that funded for fiscal year 23, just because there's so many urgent and exciting plans that have been advanced by this administration that I, like Councilor Sander said, really just dying to work with the administration to accelerate, to work on.

1813
02:37:58,170 --> 02:38:06,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Short of that, I think that putting a line item into this budget for the zoning consultant is a good plan B to make sure that that space is being held.

1814
02:38:07,391 --> 02:38:11,312
[SPEAKER_01]: But I just wanted to really take the opportunity to reemphasize that.

1815
02:38:12,132 --> 02:38:23,383
[SPEAKER_01]: if we want to catch up on development, if we want to catch up on housing, if we want to, you know, compete to be a leader in climate, all these things that we really can't afford to wait on, this is something that we need to do that.

1816
02:38:23,763 --> 02:38:24,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Thanks.

1817
02:38:24,684 --> 02:38:25,785
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Councillor Collins.

1818
02:38:25,825 --> 02:38:33,473
[SPEAKER_12]: On the motion of Councillor Graviello, seconded by Councillor Collins, Mr. Clerk, are you having the motion, Mr. Clerk?

1819
02:38:34,081 --> 02:38:34,781
[SPEAKER_07]: I have the motion.

1820
02:38:34,801 --> 02:38:40,605
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Kerrio moved to request that the administration provide some money for a zoning consultant in the FY 2023 budget.

1821
02:38:40,985 --> 02:38:42,526
[SPEAKER_07]: And I have a second from Councilor Collins.

1822
02:38:43,806 --> 02:38:44,287
[SPEAKER_12]: Yes, I will.

1823
02:38:44,367 --> 02:38:47,268
[SPEAKER_12]: I will change the word some money to the word funding, if you don't mind.

1824
02:38:47,288 --> 02:38:48,829
[SPEAKER_07]: Provide some funding.

1825
02:38:49,069 --> 02:38:49,410
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay.

1826
02:38:51,331 --> 02:38:54,993
[SPEAKER_12]: And I would take out the word some if you don't mind.

1827
02:38:55,033 --> 02:38:55,333
[SPEAKER_12]: Funding.

1828
02:38:55,573 --> 02:38:55,713
[SPEAKER_12]: Yes.

1829
02:39:04,915 --> 02:39:05,996
[SPEAKER_12]: All those in favor?

1830
02:39:06,176 --> 02:39:06,336
[SPEAKER_11]: Aye.

1831
02:39:06,736 --> 02:39:07,196
[SPEAKER_12]: Opposed?

1832
02:39:07,716 --> 02:39:08,577
[SPEAKER_12]: Motion passes.

1833
02:39:10,558 --> 02:39:12,459
[SPEAKER_12]: Any further discussion on the legislative budget?

1834
02:39:12,539 --> 02:39:15,440
[SPEAKER_12]: I have one item if my fellow councilors don't have any.

1835
02:39:17,181 --> 02:39:23,625
[SPEAKER_12]: Madam Chief of Staff, I know we budgeted, the budget is $9,600 for our videographer.

1836
02:39:25,225 --> 02:39:28,247
[SPEAKER_12]: And I understand that there's some questions around

1837
02:39:29,728 --> 02:39:31,209
[SPEAKER_12]: compensation and things like that.

1838
02:39:31,349 --> 02:39:40,617
[SPEAKER_12]: I just want to note that whereas Mr. Colbert, our videographer is a contracted employee, he's a contractor.

1839
02:39:40,657 --> 02:39:42,458
[SPEAKER_12]: He's not a W-2 employee of the city.

1840
02:39:44,280 --> 02:39:51,726
[SPEAKER_12]: What would be the process for us to request that he be paid at a rate of $500 per month instead of $350 per month?

1841
02:39:51,766 --> 02:39:54,728
[SPEAKER_12]: That would still bring us in significantly below the $9,600 budget.

1842
02:39:58,379 --> 02:39:59,540
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Vice President Behrs.

1843
02:40:00,140 --> 02:40:09,606
[SPEAKER_05]: This budget does include, because it had the space, a adjustment to the videographer to $400 a month.

1844
02:40:11,227 --> 02:40:19,292
[SPEAKER_05]: It's important, again, to evaluate the positions and evaluate the costs and increases and make them reasonably consistent across the organization.

1845
02:40:19,813 --> 02:40:23,375
[SPEAKER_05]: The city has not settled on collective bargaining agreements as of yet.

1846
02:40:23,903 --> 02:40:35,325
[SPEAKER_05]: I realize that this is a smaller stipend position, so to speak, or is a smaller contracted position, so to speak, but it's one that we look at carefully.

1847
02:40:37,445 --> 02:40:46,267
[SPEAKER_05]: And as well as a messenger report, a messenger report is proposed under this budget to go up to $550 per month.

1848
02:40:48,487 --> 02:40:53,508
[SPEAKER_05]: Based on the city council's requests, we did attempt to make changes to those two.

1849
02:40:54,871 --> 02:41:03,497
[SPEAKER_12]: Um, just, um, so I'm seeing under purchase of service videographer that we're budgeted at 9,600 for the year.

1850
02:41:05,139 --> 02:41:06,720
[SPEAKER_12]: Uh, $400 per month would only be 4,800.

1851
02:41:07,000 --> 02:41:15,226
[SPEAKER_12]: Um, so is it possible for us to move up beyond that given the space in the budget?

1852
02:41:17,252 --> 02:41:21,213
[SPEAKER_05]: Vice President Bairds, I'd have to look to see if there were any other costs that were applied to that line.

1853
02:41:22,893 --> 02:41:26,354
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't jump out at me at the moment, but I'd have to take a look to see that.

1854
02:41:26,594 --> 02:41:36,836
[SPEAKER_05]: But again, it comes back to ensuring fairness across the organization where other employees are not being provided at this point in time adjustments because contracts haven't been settled.

1855
02:41:38,736 --> 02:41:41,317
[SPEAKER_05]: And largely negotiations are just starting.

1856
02:41:42,617 --> 02:41:46,038
[SPEAKER_12]: I hear you, but these are certainly, Mr. Colbert,

1857
02:41:47,560 --> 02:41:51,043
[SPEAKER_12]: contracted non-union position that's a monthly stipend.

1858
02:41:51,844 --> 02:41:53,526
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, we don't provide him benefits.

1859
02:41:53,606 --> 02:41:54,867
[SPEAKER_12]: We don't take taxes out.

1860
02:41:55,167 --> 02:41:56,869
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, it's all on him.

1861
02:41:56,889 --> 02:42:02,414
[SPEAKER_12]: I personally, I understand the work that you do on personnel.

1862
02:42:02,734 --> 02:42:08,920
[SPEAKER_12]: I just personally don't see how this impacts, you know, a negotiation on a multimillion dollar contract.

1863
02:42:08,960 --> 02:42:11,963
[SPEAKER_12]: You know, we're talking about a difference of, you know,

1864
02:42:13,332 --> 02:42:17,936
[SPEAKER_12]: like the change in longevity or the change in sick leave buyback or for another department.

1865
02:42:19,017 --> 02:42:20,358
[SPEAKER_12]: So I appreciate the increase to 400.

1866
02:42:20,919 --> 02:42:36,432
[SPEAKER_12]: I think that of course, Mr. Colbert will appreciate that, but I would respectfully request and if councilor could make a motion that a motion of councilor Caravaglia to request that.

1867
02:42:37,473 --> 02:42:40,716
[SPEAKER_06]: People forget, he's been here about three nights this week.

1868
02:42:41,670 --> 02:42:43,790
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you know, he works more than his fair share.

1869
02:42:43,810 --> 02:42:49,692
[SPEAKER_06]: So I think that's, I think he, I think it's well-deserved.

1870
02:42:49,852 --> 02:42:51,032
[SPEAKER_06]: Would you like to comment on this?

1871
02:42:52,272 --> 02:42:53,032
[SPEAKER_12]: No, okay.

1872
02:42:54,373 --> 02:43:02,434
[SPEAKER_12]: On the motion of Councilor Carvillo, seconded by Councilor Sang to request an increase to $500 a week in stipend, $500 a month in stipend.

1873
02:43:02,454 --> 02:43:04,535
[SPEAKER_12]: Jeez, I can't, it doesn't even sound right.

1874
02:43:04,555 --> 02:43:05,995
[SPEAKER_12]: $500 a month in stipend for our videographer,

1875
02:43:10,932 --> 02:43:13,293
[SPEAKER_12]: Mr. Colbert, all those in favor?

1876
02:43:14,233 --> 02:43:14,734
[SPEAKER_12]: Opposed?

1877
02:43:15,054 --> 02:43:15,994
[SPEAKER_12]: Motion passes.

1878
02:43:18,635 --> 02:43:20,116
[SPEAKER_12]: Any further discussion on the legislative?

1879
02:43:21,636 --> 02:43:23,917
[SPEAKER_12]: All right, any discussion from the public on the legislative?

1880
02:43:25,638 --> 02:43:28,719
[SPEAKER_12]: Seeing none, we'll move to the executive, our final budget of the night.

1881
02:43:31,480 --> 02:43:36,442
[SPEAKER_12]: Madam Chief of Staff, I'm happy to turn it over to you for a presentation.

1882
02:43:37,858 --> 02:43:40,080
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you very much, Vice President bears.

1883
02:43:40,740 --> 02:43:50,709
[SPEAKER_05]: So by and large, there really aren't material changes to this budget with one exception, the negotiated salaries line has increased over last year's number.

1884
02:43:51,207 --> 02:43:56,630
[SPEAKER_05]: And that is because there are two contracts in particular that have been expired for a period of time.

1885
02:43:56,650 --> 02:44:00,813
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the clerical contract and the fire union contract in particular.

1886
02:44:02,113 --> 02:44:11,279
[SPEAKER_05]: At this point, those are just estimated numbers so that we can attempt to meet the obligations of prior years during negotiation processes.

1887
02:44:12,129 --> 02:44:30,805
[SPEAKER_05]: And the personnel line item is going down because we did have a transition in our director of communications position where the former director of communications stepped into a more part-time role and the COVID-19 public information officer became the director of communications.

1888
02:44:30,885 --> 02:44:34,348
[SPEAKER_05]: And there's a modification in that wage.

1889
02:44:34,428 --> 02:44:36,109
[SPEAKER_05]: There's a difference in experience there.

1890
02:44:38,592 --> 02:44:40,073
[SPEAKER_05]: Other than that, by and large,

1891
02:44:41,332 --> 02:44:45,996
[SPEAKER_05]: The majority of the impact, as I mentioned under personnel, is negotiated salaries.

1892
02:44:46,576 --> 02:44:55,303
[SPEAKER_05]: Under ordinary expenses, it's basically flat, consistent with what we've been presenting or attempting to present with many other, if not all, budgets.

1893
02:44:56,124 --> 02:44:56,444
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

1894
02:44:57,805 --> 02:44:58,666
[SPEAKER_12]: Thank you, Madam Chair and staff.

1895
02:44:58,706 --> 02:44:59,787
[SPEAKER_12]: Questions from the council?

1896
02:45:05,071 --> 02:45:07,073
[SPEAKER_12]: I have a question if my fellow, oh, Councilor Collins.

1897
02:45:10,497 --> 02:45:24,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Vice President bears, I note that the line item for translation services has been reduced to zero and the proposed fiscal year for 23 budget I'm wondering if the chief of staff of mine speaking to that.

1898
02:45:26,277 --> 02:45:26,657
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

1899
02:45:27,057 --> 02:45:33,060
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, Vice President Behrs, through you to Councilor Collins, I think that's a great question, and I'm glad you picked up on that.

1900
02:45:33,440 --> 02:45:40,204
[SPEAKER_05]: It is one that we were planning to hopefully transfer to the diversity, equity, and inclusion budget.

1901
02:45:40,624 --> 02:45:53,970
[SPEAKER_05]: However, given the tightness of the budget and understanding that ARPA could be used for these types of costs, are going to be placing the majority of those costs in that same ballpark under ARPA.

1902
02:45:59,410 --> 02:45:59,731
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

1903
02:46:00,912 --> 02:46:01,752
[SPEAKER_12]: Any other questions?

1904
02:46:01,793 --> 02:46:02,633
[SPEAKER_12]: Councilor Carvio.

